{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In these last two examples, we could actually have done some calculation in order to check whether or not the two events are independent. Sometimes we can just use common sense to guide us as to whether two events are independent. Here is an example.","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"rxjdbAEL70"}],"key":"lQEszHN56E"}],"class":"tip","key":"vd9Xw7tJsz"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Blue Eyes in a Family","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"GH0f82Az4b"}],"key":"E5sTOcepTe"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A family has 4 children, two of whom are selected at random. Let B1 be the event that one child has blue eyes, and B2 be the event that the other chosen child has blue eyes. In this case, B1 and B2 are not independent, since we know that eye color is hereditary, so whether or not one child is blue-eyed will increase or decrease the chances that the other child has blue eyes, respectively.","position":{"start":{"line":74,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"key":"LY4sbKfmRL"}],"key":"yQPDmCsURy"}],"class":"tip","key":"QEGT8miePg"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Blue Eyes in a Large Population","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"DhlsdhBDQP"}],"key":"HH0mjyeQV7"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two people are selected at random from all people in the United States. Let B1 be the event that one of the people has blue eyes and B2 be the event that the other person has blue eyes. In this case, since they were chosen at random, whether one of them has blue eyes has no effect on the likelihood that the other one has blue eyes, and therefore B1 and B2 are independent.","position":{"start":{"line":80,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"key":"gz0HesBWE9"}],"key":"d0qTamc3HQ"}],"class":"tip","key":"ZjXZ47gXyy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note:","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"smtdtTjVKO"}],"key":"tt0hXU1ehT"}],"key":"Gwae5GpQYe"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can generalize what we learned in the last example and say that when two individuals are selected at random from a large population (like in the example, the entire U.S.), any event associated with one individual is independent of any event associated with the other individual. The fact that the two are chosen from a large population is key to the independence.","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"pEwiMuS5ci"}],"key":"p2zmvpOoEJ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":87,"column":1},"end":{"line":87,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we were to change the example to: There are 10 people in a room, 4 of whom have blue eyes. Two people are chosen at random. Let B1 be the event that the first person has blue eyes and let B2 be the event that the second person has blue eyes. In this case, since the two are chosen from a group of only 10 (rather than a large population) the events B1 and B2 are not independent. Clearly, whether or not the first person has blue eyes (i.e., whether or not B1 occurs) does have an effect on whether B2 occurs. You will get more practice on this point in the activities below the next comment.","position":{"start":{"line":87,"column":1},"end":{"line":87,"column":1}},"key":"RWuYMjWUUs"}],"key":"eUX4vrRp9o"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":89,"column":1},"end":{"line":89,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disjoint vs. Independent Events","position":{"start":{"line":89,"column":1},"end":{"line":89,"column":1}},"key":"KqDHom221S"}],"identifier":"disjoint-vs-independent-events","label":"Disjoint vs. Independent Events","html_id":"disjoint-vs-independent-events","implicit":true,"key":"UtDjnXvPIM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is quite common for students to initially get confused about the distinction between the idea of ","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"vLJNMjKjdk"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"disjoint events","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"ED8CaMyGsw"}],"key":"J8gtwYSwkw"},{"type":"text","value":" and the idea of ","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"kvhA4k2jql"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"independent events","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"mSegNMYol9"}],"key":"yLHEgLejkm"},{"type":"text","value":". The purpose of this comment (and the activity that follows it) is to help students develop more understanding about these very different ideas.","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"Tqtl8RxJrr"}],"key":"UKtZf9nk4d"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The idea of ","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"key":"unG8vBYYsH"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"disjoint events","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"key":"YZz5wdeK82"}],"key":"fuMvd9M6Ma"},{"type":"text","value":" is about whether or not it is possible for the events to occur at the same time.","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"key":"IEpQkhYAaH"}],"key":"QoxDZbI1Sv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The idea of ","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"key":"x6vLPNPW5g"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"independent events","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"key":"zuSEjW8qug"}],"key":"Dlr9oY9itW"},{"type":"text","value":" is about whether or not the events affect each other in the sense that the occurrence of one event affects the probability of the occurrence of the other.","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"key":"dlkcVRENlB"}],"key":"hCZOyA0pTZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":97,"column":1},"end":{"line":97,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In each of the following questions, you are presented with a random experiment and two events related to it. You are asked to decide whether the events are disjoint or not, and whether the events are independent or not.","position":{"start":{"line":97,"column":1},"end":{"line":97,"column":1}},"key":"jjfBKDXXaY"}],"key":"zd6gzqjraQ"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30b7ab02eb92796a87df491e36b218c4.mjs","model":{"question":"A card is drawn at random from a standard deck. A: \"the card is a heart.\" B: \"the card is a king.\" Are A and B disjoint? Are they independent?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Not disjoint, and independent","feedback":"Correct! The king of hearts belongs to both events, so they are not disjoint. And P(king) = 4/52 = 1/13, while P(king given heart) = 1/13 also—knowing the suit doesn't change the chance of a king, so they are independent."},{"correct":false,"text":"Not disjoint, and not independent","feedback":"They are not disjoint (the king of hearts is in both), and check the probabilities: knowing the card is a heart leaves the chance of a king at 1/13, unchanged."},{"correct":false,"text":"Disjoint, and not independent","feedback":"They can occur together (the king of hearts), so they are not disjoint."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The king of hearts is both a heart and a king. And does knowing the card is a heart change the chance it is a king?","explanation":""},"key":"sZXNGLoVnb"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30b7ab02eb92796a87df491e36b218c4.mjs","model":{"question":"One student is chosen at random from a class. A: \"the student is a freshman.\" B: \"the student is a sophomore.\" Are A and B disjoint? Are they independent?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Not disjoint, and not independent","feedback":"They cannot occur together, so they ARE disjoint—and that's exactly why they are dependent."},{"correct":true,"text":"Disjoint, and not independent","feedback":"Correct! A student cannot be both a freshman and a sophomore (disjoint), and knowing A occurred drops the probability of B to 0, so they are not independent."},{"correct":false,"text":"Disjoint, and independent","feedback":"Disjoint events can never be independent—knowing one occurred changes the other's probability to 0."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Can one student be both? And if you learn the student is a freshman, what is the chance they are a sophomore?","explanation":""},"key":"uojwROjhE9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":119,"column":1},"end":{"line":119,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s summarize with a table of the possible combinations:","position":{"start":{"line":119,"column":1},"end":{"line":119,"column":1}},"key":"KOn3BLFAX8"}],"key":"XEPnWRHxLG"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[],"key":"aW26zljYMx"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A and B Independent","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"key":"FqiQBaENi8"}],"key":"U4vEEQP00X"}],"key":"JnRw451GqM"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A and B Not Independent","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"key":"y8Z8C1e2jT"}],"key":"fBQ5H9X0UW"}],"key":"eQHoLHfOuV"}],"key":"VoD3YglatE"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A and B Disjoint","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"key":"kmYaIl0kC2"}],"key":"ugt19s2DDp"}],"key":"Mb2WWuvYQH"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"DOES NOT EXIST","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"key":"oeZqlGQO7a"}],"key":"vHjG5Xd7eA"}],"key":"VpScAt2cKw"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"possible","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":123,"column":1}},"key":"kkNfsGJraE"}],"key":"gXMw9VXX4y"}],"key":"x0mgVzN6jH"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A and B Not Disjoint","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"key":"QDPIvxhvT7"}],"key":"w70wWjk54M"}],"key":"ADZxsn6JGT"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"possible","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"key":"P64rZRzm6z"}],"key":"CIe2mrAuUm"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"possible","position":{"start":{"line":124,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"key":"iY0ByhT6AL"}],"key":"eTAbUQS5o8"}],"key":"CxUPex4NnB"}],"key":"BkdtAB0okE"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":126,"column":1},"end":{"line":126,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why is the case “disjoint and independent” impossible?","position":{"start":{"line":126,"column":1},"end":{"line":126,"column":1}},"key":"kQjeQSgwd5"}],"key":"VPMXR403hq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall: A and B disjoint means that they cannot happen together. In other words, A and B disjoint implies that if event A occurs then B does not, and vice versa. Well, if that’s the case, knowing that event A has occurred dramatically changes the likelihood that event B occurs—that likelihood is 0. This implies that A and B are not independent.","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"key":"tLCyxHrlkB"}],"key":"FPpJjr3SZt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":130,"column":1},"end":{"line":130,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we understand the idea of independent events, we can finally get to rule 5. As mentioned before, Rule 5 actually has two versions, one for finding P(A and B) in the special case in which the events A and B are independent, and a more general version for use when the events are not necessarily independent. We will first present the version of rule 5 that is restricted to independent events, and in the next module we will revisit Rule 5 and present the more general version.","position":{"start":{"line":130,"column":1},"end":{"line":130,"column":1}},"key":"bBEvDrhllh"}],"key":"npWAuDFmeH"}],"key":"xg0ObzSrOM"}],"key":"vlxS7wFHUo"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Addition Rule for Disjoint Events: Finding P(A or B)","url":"/pages/m09-probability-rules-4-of-11","group":"Section 6: Finding Probability of Events"},"next":{"title":"The Multiplication Rule for Independent Events: Finding P(A and B)","url":"/pages/m09-probability-rules-6-of-11","group":"Section 6: Finding Probability of Events"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}